00:02:59.259 --> 00:03:01.740
Okay, let's begin.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:07.899
Okay, let me set the stage why this conversation even matters.

00:03:07.899 --> 00:03:14.619
Well, companies face genuinely challenging environment right now.

00:03:14.619 --> 00:03:17.860
And those challenges could be grouped into three categories.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:21.220
The first one is around conscious consumers.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:29.380
According to Deloitte's research, 47% of modern consumers consider themselves as value seekers.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:35.779
And that means that attracting those consumers require deeper understanding of their needs,

00:03:35.779 --> 00:03:43.259
their specific behavior, predicting their churn, and then addressing it with more targeted,

00:03:43.259 --> 00:03:46.699
more personalized campaigns.

00:03:46.699 --> 00:03:55.660
Luckily, we currently have access to abandoned consumer data and AI technology that allows

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:04.899
us transition from the traditional retail marketing into more personalized, more value-based

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.979
agentic marketing.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:10.020
The second large category is operations.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:17.380
Obviously, in the current geopolitical environment and tariff wars, there are lots of existing

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.420
supply chains got disrupted.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:30.059
But again, with AI and data technology, we're getting access to the intelligence that allows

00:04:30.059 --> 00:04:40.179
us to build unified planning systems and monitor supply chains end-to-end, and also

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:46.220
enable almost real-time optimization of the store operations.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.459
And the last big bucket is agentic commerce.

00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:58.820
Agentic commerce promises the similar scale, the magnitude of change to what we saw back

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:04.179
in 1990s with the emergence of internet and online shopping.

00:05:04.179 --> 00:05:08.700
And now lots of retailers, they're trying to figure out how to optimize their product

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:20.380
catalogs and build more personalized offerings to win attention of those shopping agents.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.339
And you can see that the challenges are very different, but the way we address them is

00:05:24.339 --> 00:05:26.220
very similar.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:36.100
We're using data and we're using AI technology to enable what we call agentic enterprise

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:45.779
in retail, an organization where agents work alongside with humans to scale operations

00:05:45.779 --> 00:05:47.859
and to multiply productivity.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:59.940
On the other side, what we see a lot, organizations getting super excited about agents and they

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.019
try to deploy them at scale.

00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:15.619
And it's one thing when you're using enterprise license for charge GPT and enable all your

00:06:15.619 --> 00:06:22.420
employees with productivity tools, like so they can create their slides or draft their

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:27.059
emails or even put Python codes.

00:06:27.059 --> 00:06:33.019
That's a completely different thing when you are trying to embed those agents to autonomously

00:06:33.019 --> 00:06:37.380
drive your enterprise workflows.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.739
Many leaders still believe that agents are smart enough, so they will figure out your

00:06:41.739 --> 00:06:48.540
process, they will find the right data and they will basically do the right thing.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.820
And this is very expensive way to fail.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.779
Agents they don't figure out the messy data architecture.

00:06:59.779 --> 00:07:07.260
They cannot understand, they will try hard, they will burn a lot of tokens, but they can

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.739
still make mistakes.

00:07:09.739 --> 00:07:12.380
Here is an example of the test I did myself.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:24.739
I asked an AI agent to assemble customer 360 profile using a raw bronze layer data and

00:07:24.739 --> 00:07:34.220
then compare it to already existing profile in the gold layer, clean data.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:42.820
So obviously it took about 10x more time for agent to rebuild, reconstruct this profile

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:47.579
and it burned around 9x more tokens.

00:07:47.579 --> 00:07:50.299
But this is something that I expected.

00:07:50.299 --> 00:07:57.859
Something that I didn't expect was that the result was actually different.

00:07:57.859 --> 00:08:04.820
Because I didn't define a specific parameter lifetime value for the customer, so agent

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:11.820
when it calculated it, it subtracted all the return transactions from this lifetime value.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.339
And this is not what we've done for the gold layer.

00:08:14.339 --> 00:08:17.140
So I got a different result.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:23.380
And the lesson from this exercise is that if you run your agents on a raw unprepared

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:34.739
data without the proper semantics and the business context, you will pretty much spend

00:08:34.739 --> 00:08:43.299
a lot of effort and money and scale your mess.

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:51.099
This is why at Snowflake we recommend the following architecture to build your agentic

00:08:51.099 --> 00:08:53.219
enterprise in retail.

00:08:53.219 --> 00:08:58.539
Before you get to this top action layer where your agents operate, you need to enable three

00:08:58.539 --> 00:09:06.659
layers underneath, starting with unified data layer that is open, interoperable, supports

00:09:06.659 --> 00:09:09.659
both structured and unstructured data.

00:09:09.659 --> 00:09:19.940
And then you enable governance layer, which doesn't only control access to data for humans

00:09:20.099 --> 00:09:26.299
and AI agents, but also helps control agents' costs.

00:09:26.299 --> 00:09:32.619
And then the context layer, the one that defines business rules across different use cases,

00:09:32.619 --> 00:09:38.659
across different functions, customer 360 and personalization for marketing, product catalog,

00:09:38.659 --> 00:09:46.580
JT Commerce for merchandising, supply chain, store operations, financial planning for different

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:48.780
business functions.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:57.419
Snowflake platform provides building blocks for all those layers and also powers our partners

00:09:57.419 --> 00:10:02.619
like Posit to build applications natively on Snowflake.

00:10:12.619 --> 00:10:15.580
Okay, now let's talk about Brilliant Earth's story.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.580
Natalia, Matias, please take it away.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.580
Thank you so much, Sam.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:27.619
So we've talked about the platform, what runs inside of Snowflake, how Posit team fits there

00:10:27.619 --> 00:10:29.940
with a native app.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.900
That covers what's possible.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.260
What we want to do now is to show you what it looks like when a real team uses it.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.780
We're going to follow Brilliant Earth, many of you know them as the fine jewelry company

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.419
with a strong e-commerce business and a growing network of showrooms.

00:10:45.419 --> 00:10:52.059
So Matias, their data science and insight team lead has been on this journey with us.

00:10:52.059 --> 00:10:58.340
And the best place to start it is not the architecture diagram necessary.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:10:59.780
It's where they began.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:04.979
So because of, I suspect a lot of you will find yourself in the same spot.

00:11:04.979 --> 00:11:12.020
So we wanted to follow the journey from where the issues arose and how the solution became

00:11:12.179 --> 00:11:15.219
available with Snowflake and Posit together.

00:11:23.859 --> 00:11:29.140
So before any transformation, Brilliant Earth's bottleneck fell into two groups.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.580
One on the left side were the tools.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.900
One on the right side were the people and processes.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:41.219
The second group mattered as much because better tools as Posit and Snowflake would not

00:11:41.219 --> 00:11:43.700
have fixed the issues by itself.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.780
So let's start with the tools on the left side.

00:11:46.739 --> 00:11:50.340
First of all, the issues were the siloed legacy tools.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.739
The data science happened in silos.

00:11:52.739 --> 00:11:58.500
Different teams used different numbers, different copy of the numbers, and there was not a single

00:11:58.500 --> 00:11:59.380
side of truth.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.140
So this is where the Snowflake came in.

00:12:02.419 --> 00:12:07.700
The reports were disagreed and someone had to spend time working out which one was right.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.659
A lot of the work was manual, right?

00:12:10.659 --> 00:12:16.260
So oftentimes, the work depended on one person's steps on one machine.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:17.780
This doesn't scale.

00:12:18.419 --> 00:12:19.700
This doesn't reproduce.

00:12:20.739 --> 00:12:26.580
Also, a lot of the teams, you know, we all have our preferred tooling, especially when

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:27.780
it comes to data scientists.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:33.460
So individual teams trying to fix those issues brought individual tools to solve those individual

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:33.940
problems.

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:41.859
Each made sense on its own, but when it brings together, they cannot align into one platform

00:12:41.859 --> 00:12:44.179
that the whole company can use.

00:12:45.219 --> 00:12:52.419
And then with AI, the issue arose where can we future-proof those solutions, right?

00:12:52.419 --> 00:12:53.460
It works today.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.940
Somehow we patch it together, but will it work in a year or in two?

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:02.500
So then those were the issues with the tools.

00:13:03.059 --> 00:13:08.820
But when it came to the people as well, there was a lot of the hand-built reporting.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:15.059
So the reports, weekly showroom and e-commerce reports were built by hand every week.

00:13:15.619 --> 00:13:16.580
The time passes.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:18.260
The report was ready by the end of the week.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:24.020
By the next week, the numbers were already not the ones that the leadership wanted to

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.179
see or the ones that you can action upon.

00:13:26.739 --> 00:13:28.340
The deliverables were static.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:31.460
We're going to talk about those use cases later on.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:41.059
But again, this didn't help to have the static HTML for the team that could just read it,

00:13:41.059 --> 00:13:43.780
but not ask any additional questions.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:48.979
But when the questions were asked, they were asked from the one central team serving every

00:13:48.979 --> 00:13:49.940
department.

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:56.900
So however good that team was, and going back to Matthias, however good that team was, it's

00:13:56.900 --> 00:13:57.619
a ceiling.

00:13:58.099 --> 00:14:04.260
Every request became a prioritization debate, and someone always loses in this sense.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:08.020
So putting these two columns together, you can see the real problem.

00:14:08.739 --> 00:14:11.859
The issue was not a shortage of data or talent.

00:14:11.859 --> 00:14:17.619
It's just putting all these things together and provide them to work in a reproducible

00:14:17.619 --> 00:14:17.859
way.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:23.059
With that, Matthias, do you have anything to add to the issues that I stated?

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:24.979
Oh, goodness.

00:14:25.859 --> 00:14:28.020
No, I think that summarizes it really well.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:35.140
I think we've had, and we still have, incredible talent and brilliant earth.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.460
Some of the best people I've ever worked with work here.

00:14:38.979 --> 00:14:44.739
And it was really an exercise in trying to bring people into the same ecosystem and the

00:14:44.739 --> 00:14:48.500
same sort of platform to really harness all of that talent.

00:14:49.059 --> 00:14:56.659
And I think we've been able to build something pretty incredible over the last few months,

00:14:56.659 --> 00:14:57.219
I would say.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:05.859
And all of those issues that you mentioned have definitely been heavily reduced, if not

00:15:05.859 --> 00:15:06.419
diminished.

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.659
Like you said, we're going to talk about a few of those.

00:15:10.659 --> 00:15:17.619
But yeah, holistically, I think this explains it decently well, yeah.

00:15:18.979 --> 00:15:19.460
Thank you.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:24.580
I know sometimes it helps to just take a step back and for someone outsider to look at it

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:26.099
and bring them all together.

00:15:26.099 --> 00:15:33.219
So from that standpoint, was there a specific project or a moment in time when you decided

00:15:33.219 --> 00:15:38.099
all of these little issues combined into the bigger problem that you cannot fix incrementally?

00:15:40.179 --> 00:15:41.380
That's a great question.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:47.859
I would say, so I'm not sure if there was like a specific project that spurred all of

00:15:47.859 --> 00:15:48.359
this.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:54.900
I will say that I think with the advent of AI that we're all obviously in at this point,

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:00.900
I think it became increasingly clear that the current path that we were on wasn't the

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:06.739
one that I think would put us into the AI future that we wanted to be a part of.

00:16:08.419 --> 00:16:11.859
We are very fast paced here.

00:16:11.859 --> 00:16:14.500
We do act on trends really rapidly.

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:19.619
And we want the same to be true for our data science and our analytics.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:24.419
So it was very important to us to kind of have a platform that supported that.

00:16:25.299 --> 00:16:30.820
And I think we just weren't entirely confident or we became increasingly more

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:39.059
inconfident, I should say, that the sort of AI solution that we had in place may not be

00:16:39.059 --> 00:16:43.460
that one that we feel like is going to take us there.

00:16:43.619 --> 00:16:46.900
So that's why we took off on this path.

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:49.219
All right.

00:16:49.219 --> 00:16:50.179
Thank you.

00:16:50.179 --> 00:16:53.539
So from the issues, let's move into solutions.

00:16:53.539 --> 00:16:55.299
This is, I think, the most exciting part.

00:16:59.299 --> 00:17:00.020
All right.

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:03.940
So here's what BrilliantOS set out to change.

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:09.459
Again, they didn't bring a separate tool for each team.

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:17.219
Showrooms, merchandising, digital growth, and finance now serve from one deployment

00:17:17.219 --> 00:17:18.660
running inside of Snowflake.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:23.780
And let me walk you through the three examples that Mathias shared with us.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:28.099
So the first one, this is for the growth team.

00:17:28.979 --> 00:17:33.780
So remember, I've mentioned before those static multi-page marketing mix reports.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:38.819
Now it's a Bayesian model written in Python and executed in Snowflake

00:17:38.819 --> 00:17:42.660
with an interactive Streamlit app on Posit Connect in front of it.

00:17:43.459 --> 00:17:47.219
Now the VP of growth doesn't have to just read the results anymore, right?

00:17:47.219 --> 00:17:48.339
It's not static.

00:17:48.339 --> 00:17:56.660
They can plan the media mix themselves, combining this domain knowledge with the correct tools.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:18:02.579
And again, they can ask questions without waiting for the data science team to answer

00:18:02.579 --> 00:18:03.380
them for them.

00:18:04.339 --> 00:18:09.380
So this one, this was the first use case that was shared.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:12.739
The second one was the showroom operations, right?

00:18:12.739 --> 00:18:17.619
So we learned that more than a hundred users moved into governed reporting now, right?

00:18:17.619 --> 00:18:24.260
Remember before they all shared this, they all used those different tools themselves.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:31.219
The number didn't match, but now was, again, in a controlled environment with the permissions

00:18:31.219 --> 00:18:32.739
inherited from Snowflake.

00:18:33.619 --> 00:18:36.979
Nobody has to rebuild the access rules and the second tool.

00:18:37.859 --> 00:18:40.180
It all can happen inside of Snowflake.

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:45.300
They can utilize the same tooling, the same data, all within the control environment.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.699
And most of all, they can share it as well.

00:18:48.819 --> 00:18:54.819
The third one, and this one answers the hand-built weekly reports as well.

00:18:55.699 --> 00:18:56.739
The last part of it.

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:02.260
So the metrics are computed in code against Snowflake.

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:08.579
So the numbers are reproducible, but then it goes into the code API that writes a narrative

00:19:08.579 --> 00:19:09.859
on top of them.

00:19:09.859 --> 00:19:12.739
And then the report is published in Posit Connect.

00:19:12.739 --> 00:19:18.420
Again, in all three use cases, and this is what amazed me the most about the whole story

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:24.660
with Brilliant Earth, is that different departments, very similar problems, actually, very similar

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:30.260
issues, they all found a solution in one way or another utilizing the same tools.

00:19:31.219 --> 00:19:33.699
So I've mentioned three use cases.

00:19:33.699 --> 00:19:36.979
Matheus, do you want to add anything else to that?

00:19:36.979 --> 00:19:41.859
Are there any other areas where the Snowflake is used, Posit is used?

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:43.939
There are many.

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:49.219
You know, I would say, holistically, it's just a very versatile system.

00:19:49.219 --> 00:19:55.219
And I think the reason why those are the sort of three examples that I shared with you when

00:19:55.219 --> 00:20:02.579
we first talked about making this webinar was because it was showcasing how we're using

00:20:02.579 --> 00:20:06.900
this sort of ecosystem of products in very different ways.

00:20:07.859 --> 00:20:08.819
And you're absolutely right.

00:20:08.819 --> 00:20:15.219
So the marketing mix model, for example, that model already existed as a Bayesian model

00:20:15.219 --> 00:20:22.020
before, but it was sort of a more siloed process where a data scientist was in charge of running

00:20:22.099 --> 00:20:29.060
it and sort of pushing it out to the team, whereas now we've gotten like a fully interactive

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:33.780
data product that the team can actually interact with and run scenarios with and all of those

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:34.339
different things.

00:20:36.099 --> 00:20:40.099
And on the other use cases, it's sort of the same thing.

00:20:40.099 --> 00:20:48.099
We're really trying to sort of figure out how we can take the sort of good work that

00:20:48.979 --> 00:20:54.979
we can do on the data science end and put that into the hands of the experts that we

