00:40:53.501 --> 00:40:59.981
So, it's more than a simple migration from sort of one BI tool into another BI tool,

00:40:59.981 --> 00:41:00.780
if that makes sense.

00:41:02.861 --> 00:41:08.381
I think we have another question, which is similar to, you covered it a little bit,

00:41:09.101 --> 00:41:14.061
but is there a particular reason why I decided to move from the Posit side?

00:41:14.061 --> 00:41:15.181
I'm curious as well.

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:21.981
I, when you move to the native app versus just a separate box,

00:41:21.981 --> 00:41:24.220
separate server deployment or in cloud deployment,

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:26.301
what was the reason behind it?

00:41:26.301 --> 00:41:28.381
And did you regret that solution ever?

00:41:31.741 --> 00:41:33.340
Okay, that's a fantastic question.

00:41:34.861 --> 00:41:39.260
So, for those of the listeners that don't know,

00:41:39.260 --> 00:41:44.061
but Posit obviously comes in, let's call it different flavors.

00:41:44.061 --> 00:41:48.460
I guess, Natalia, you could probably talk much better about it than I could,

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:55.101
but from my point of view, there are different deployment methods

00:41:55.101 --> 00:41:58.061
for something like Posit Connect, for example, right?

00:41:58.061 --> 00:42:03.260
You can have it sit on like an AWS instance somewhere,

00:42:03.260 --> 00:42:07.981
or I believe there's the cloud version of it.

00:42:08.941 --> 00:42:14.140
But then there's this idea of essentially having it sit right within Snowflake

00:42:14.140 --> 00:42:15.741
as a sort of native application.

00:42:17.181 --> 00:42:20.700
I've used Posit now at multiple companies,

00:42:21.981 --> 00:42:27.260
and this is the first time that I've had the opportunity

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:29.741
of using Posit in concert with Snowflake.

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:35.580
And when I found out, and when I researched this idea of having Posit

00:42:36.541 --> 00:42:38.941
deployed as a native app inside of Snowflake,

00:42:41.501 --> 00:42:45.181
what drew me to it was really the idea of governance.

00:42:45.981 --> 00:42:53.020
So, the beautiful thing that you get with deploying Posit

00:42:53.020 --> 00:42:55.101
inside of the ecosystem of Snowflake

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:59.501
is that Snowflake still governs all of the access,

00:42:59.501 --> 00:43:00.861
all of the user roles,

00:43:00.861 --> 00:43:04.460
all of the sort of tables that someone can access.

00:43:04.541 --> 00:43:07.421
Any of those things are effectively built in,

00:43:07.421 --> 00:43:09.741
and Posit Connect can infer that,

00:43:09.741 --> 00:43:13.981
or Posit Workbench, or whatever flavor or product you end up using.

00:43:15.501 --> 00:43:23.101
And we are on a SSO provider that provisions accounts for us.

00:43:23.101 --> 00:43:29.660
So, essentially, the whole ecosystem for me as a data scientist

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:31.260
is just taken care of.

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:35.101
So, I don't have to worry about messing with permissions.

00:43:35.101 --> 00:43:41.020
My IT team loves it because, frankly, I can't mess with permissions.

00:43:41.020 --> 00:43:45.020
So, it's just sort of like a system that works really, really well.

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.981
If you're familiar with Posit Connect,

00:43:49.981 --> 00:43:52.541
there are a few downsides here and there,

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:55.260
or a few things that maybe you're used to.

00:43:55.660 --> 00:44:01.580
So, for example, on AWS, you can go straight into the config file

00:44:01.580 --> 00:44:02.861
and change some things around.

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:08.301
Those are the things you lose with Posit Connect on Snowflake.

00:44:08.301 --> 00:44:15.900
However, holistically, I have not regretted deploying it in this style for a minute.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.061
I would do it totally again like this.

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:20.541
It's been a really great system.

00:44:22.381 --> 00:44:22.881
Love it.

00:44:23.981 --> 00:44:25.101
Thank you, Matias.

00:44:25.101 --> 00:44:27.900
And thank you for highlighting the governance.

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:31.580
As we talked earlier, this is the second critical layer

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:35.580
which we need to build to enable the entire AI transformation.

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:38.541
Yeah, and it's out of the box, which is so nice about it.

00:44:40.381 --> 00:44:41.020
Great.

00:44:41.020 --> 00:44:42.061
I have one more question.

00:44:42.941 --> 00:44:47.580
How did you get business users to adopt new dashboards

00:44:47.580 --> 00:44:50.621
instead of asking for their old reports?

00:44:55.820 --> 00:45:00.381
Well, I think ultimately it's about building something

00:45:00.381 --> 00:45:04.061
that someone perceives as an improvement over what they have.

00:45:05.020 --> 00:45:07.260
Yes, we're all creatures of habit,

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:13.260
and that is potentially sometimes a blocker to adopting something new.

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:23.101
But in the case of, for example, this cohort of 100 showroom users

00:45:23.101 --> 00:45:26.381
that we migrated from the BI tool that we have

00:45:26.381 --> 00:45:30.381
to essentially a more custom solution in Positive Connect,

00:45:33.981 --> 00:45:37.181
what they used to access is essentially static reports

00:45:37.181 --> 00:45:47.421
that update over time, tabular format, customer lists, performance, tabular data.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:50.621
And that was working for them.

00:45:50.621 --> 00:45:55.580
And we essentially gave them a very similar thing on Positive Connect,

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:59.901
except we wrapped it into a package called Query Chat,

00:45:59.901 --> 00:46:04.141
which essentially allows you to take this data,

00:46:05.420 --> 00:46:12.460
put it into a format of having the information just like they were used to,

00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:18.621
but also providing a chattable experience in addition to it

00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:23.420
that's governed enough that I feel comfortable rolling it out to people.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:27.580
Where instead of having to click through 15 filters

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:29.420
to get to the view they want,

00:46:29.420 --> 00:46:36.381
they can just say, hey, I'm looking for the last 14 days

00:46:36.381 --> 00:46:40.460
of bookings performance for my showroom X.

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:45.420
And then it just filters the dashboards for them automatically.

00:46:45.661 --> 00:46:51.340
It has sort of, I would say, a limited use graphical ability,

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:53.741
so it does a little bit of visualization.

00:46:53.741 --> 00:46:58.940
And that's sort of the simple fact of it.

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:02.381
More advanced, you can also use Cortex with it

00:47:02.381 --> 00:47:07.820
so it can access your semantic layers that you've built into Cortex as agents

00:47:08.861 --> 00:47:13.021
to kind of govern this sort of chattable experience as well.

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:15.901
And those are great things.

00:47:15.901 --> 00:47:21.021
But as an end user, the great feedback that we've had

00:47:21.820 --> 00:47:25.741
and the reason why I think the adoption actually worked in this case

00:47:25.741 --> 00:47:30.781
is because a showroom operations professional,

00:47:30.781 --> 00:47:33.261
they went from having a sort of,

00:47:35.501 --> 00:47:39.101
I almost want to say Excel-like BI experience

00:47:39.741 --> 00:47:45.501
to now having a sort of more advanced experience

00:47:45.501 --> 00:47:48.220
where they still get everything that they're used to.

00:47:48.220 --> 00:47:53.021
But in addition to that, they're also getting

00:47:53.021 --> 00:47:58.940
some of those more current features set out in the market

00:47:59.501 --> 00:48:04.540
that allow me to kind of improve the experience of interacting with data.

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:08.300
All right, and thank you for mentioning Query Chat.

00:48:08.300 --> 00:48:12.460
I think one of the questions that I have for you here

00:48:12.460 --> 00:48:16.381
is about trusting AI, right?

00:48:16.381 --> 00:48:20.300
So you told us about a setup, right?

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:23.820
But the question is, how do you keep AI-generated dashboards honest?

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:25.261
Oh, goodness.

00:48:29.741 --> 00:48:32.220
I think my answer to that is that

00:48:32.781 --> 00:48:36.540
I work every day to try to make sure AI doesn't do any math

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:41.180
by giving it as many guardrails as possible.

00:48:42.781 --> 00:48:47.021
Can you, in the end, control if everyone's following those procedures?

00:48:47.021 --> 00:48:52.060
No, which is an issue, but you can try to put guardrails around it.

00:48:53.661 --> 00:48:55.420
What we've done here at Brilliant Earth

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:57.981
or what sort of I've been advocating for

00:48:58.060 --> 00:49:01.901
is that if we think of Snowflake as a sort of

00:49:01.901 --> 00:49:05.340
like beating heart of our data structure,

00:49:06.220 --> 00:49:08.861
that there are different consumption pathways

00:49:08.861 --> 00:49:12.220
that a user can choose to essentially access that data.

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:17.180
You know, there is a chattable experience, right?

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:20.060
So you can go to Snowflake Intelligence.

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:22.381
I think it's not called Snowflake Codework, I think.

00:49:23.021 --> 00:49:25.180
But you know, you can chat with data and Snowflake,

00:49:25.180 --> 00:49:28.381
and access the semantic layer and you're on your way.

00:49:29.101 --> 00:49:32.460
We've wrapped that into like Cloud as well.

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:36.940
So essentially, we have an ability to do from within Cloud,

00:49:36.940 --> 00:49:41.420
access the Snowflake semantic layer and chat with data.

00:49:42.141 --> 00:49:44.141
And we put governance around that.

00:49:44.141 --> 00:49:47.501
We write skills, we try to kind of fence it in.

00:49:47.501 --> 00:49:50.940
But in the end, it's still sort of,

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:55.580
there is always a possibility, a very real possibility

00:49:55.580 --> 00:49:57.501
that what the end user is getting

00:49:57.501 --> 00:50:03.501
is not 100% the answer that you think they should be getting.

00:50:04.141 --> 00:50:05.261
There's ways around it.

00:50:05.261 --> 00:50:09.741
You know, I know you guys just released a new package.

00:50:09.741 --> 00:50:11.261
They are the Commons package

00:50:11.261 --> 00:50:13.820
that kind of tries to work around that.

00:50:13.820 --> 00:50:15.901
But there's sort of, that's our consumption pathway.

00:50:15.901 --> 00:50:17.021
And if you think about it,

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:20.220
My thought for this pathway as a whole

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:25.580
is that like, that is sort of like an ad hoc pathway

00:50:26.300 --> 00:50:28.060
for quick and dirty analytics.

00:50:29.101 --> 00:50:33.981
Whereas things that we put on Positive Connect,

00:50:34.621 --> 00:50:36.861
we generally try to like fortify it

00:50:36.861 --> 00:50:40.861
and make sure that LLM is not the only thing

00:50:40.861 --> 00:50:42.540
that we put on Positive Connect.

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:44.700
We generally try to like fortify it

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:49.261
and make sure that LLMs are not part of the pipeline

00:50:49.261 --> 00:50:56.381
of actually writing anything that sort of in the moment.

00:50:56.381 --> 00:50:59.180
Do we use AI to write SQL code?

00:50:59.180 --> 00:51:02.141
Do we use AI to write R or Python code?

00:51:02.141 --> 00:51:02.940
Absolutely.

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:04.460
I think everyone does these days.

00:51:05.501 --> 00:51:10.300
But, you know, what we put behind our work

00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:13.501
inside of things that we have on positive,

00:51:15.261 --> 00:51:17.021
those are code-based.

00:51:17.021 --> 00:51:19.661
And I can verify, I can repeat,

00:51:20.861 --> 00:51:25.340
and I can essentially rebuild whatever is inside of there.

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:28.621
And I can trust the code.

00:51:29.661 --> 00:51:33.261
And that's sort of like how I'm thinking about it at the moment.

00:51:35.261 --> 00:51:37.021
Maybe next month is different.

00:51:37.021 --> 00:51:40.060
There's so much innovation and it's so quick

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:45.741
that at times I think it's hard to keep up.

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:49.180
So for the time being, that's sort of like where I am,

00:51:49.180 --> 00:51:52.060
but who knows what it's like in a month or two.

00:51:53.021 --> 00:51:55.981
So what are you saying is don't be afraid to try,

00:51:55.981 --> 00:51:58.141
but proceed with caution, right?

00:51:58.141 --> 00:52:00.220
I think that's a good one.

00:52:00.220 --> 00:52:02.141
I mean, like if you think about the use case

00:52:02.141 --> 00:52:04.940
that we talked about with the AI weekly reports, right?

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:09.101
Like we were trying to be intentional about like,

00:52:09.101 --> 00:52:11.261
yes, AI can summarize and, you know,

00:52:11.261 --> 00:52:12.700
it's really good at that, you know.

00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:18.220
But, you know, I'm still, you know,

00:52:18.220 --> 00:52:21.981
if anything, I want my functions to do the math.

00:52:22.540 --> 00:52:26.540
So can I maybe give AI a skill with a function?

00:52:26.540 --> 00:52:27.420
Yes, totally.

00:52:27.981 --> 00:52:31.261
Maybe that's a bit better than just flat out asking AI

00:52:31.261 --> 00:52:33.741
to do some computation for you without guardrails.

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.381
Okay, I see our time is up.

00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:40.141
So we have a few more questions,

00:52:40.141 --> 00:52:44.300
but we will answer them later over the email.

